        <body>
            <div xml:id="d41">

                <pb facs="d40-01" n="1" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76ve08/d40-01"/>

                <head>41. Telegram 29604 From the Department of State to
                    the Embassy in Bangladesh<anchor n="1" xml:id="d41-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d41-f2"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1975-02-09T04:47:00+00:00">February 9, 1975, 0447Z</date>.</dateline>
                <pb facs="d41-01" n="1" xml:id="d41-01"/>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subject:</label>
                    <item>Message for Fakhruddin Ahmed</item>
                </list>
                <list type="references">
                    <head>Ref:</head>
                    <item>Dacca 674</item>
                </list>
                <p>For Ambassador from Secretary</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>In absence of Foreign Minister Kamal
                            Hossain, please inform Foreign Secretary Fakhruddin Ahmed
                        that I discussed BDG readiness to talk about
                        normalization of relations between Pakistan and Bangladesh with Pakistani
                        Prime Minister Bhutto during his
                        Washington visit. I pointed out that your government has told us of its
                        willingness to undertake discussions without preconditions. Bhutto replied that Pakistan remains ready
                        to resume discussions with Bangladesh at any time. We did not discuss
                        substance of issues, but agreed that I would pass this message on to
                        you.</item>
                    <pb facs="d41-02" n="2" xml:id="d41-02"/>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>FYI: I did not get the impression that Bhutto plans to undertake any initiative on disputes between
                        Pakistan and Bangladesh. End FYI.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed>Kissinger</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d41-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d41-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Ford Library, National Security Adviser, Presidential Country Files for
                    Middle East and South Asia, Box 2, Bangladesh-State Department Telegrams, From
                    Secretary of State, Exdis. Confidential;
                    Immediate; Exdis. It was drafted by Griffin; cleared by NEA; and approved by Kissinger. It was repeated to Islamabad and New Delhi.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d41-f2" n="2" xml:id="d41-f2-content">
                <p>The Department transmitted Pakistani Prime Minister Zulfiqar Ali Bhutto’s reply to the Bangladeshi feeler about
                    negotiations.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
